<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perioeci</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as perioikoi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The perioeci, &quot;those dwelling around&quot;, were the second-tier citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> until about 200 BCE, free people living in several dozen dependent cities, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia. These cities ran their own domestic affairs but followed Sparta's foreign policy and sent men to its army, where they fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>; in the Spartan state they had no say. As Spartan citizens were barred from productive work, the perioeci ran trade and crafts, making the army's weapons and armour, and they alone could travel freely abroad. Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a>, they owned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name perioeci derives from the Greek peri, &quot;around&quot;, and oikos, &quot;dwelling&quot;, and roughly means those dwelling around or nearby. The polis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> was formed during the Greek Dark Ages and controlled the plains around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> river. Communities already living there that could not be assimilated into the Spartan state or subjugated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> became the perioeci. It is unknown whether they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> like the Spartans or descended from earlier populations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. Perioeci and Spartans together were called the Lakedaimonians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The perioeci were free, unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, but not full citizens. They lived in their own cities, which ancient authors described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>, and which governed their own domestic affairs under Spartan control. They had political rights only in their own city. They supplied men to the army and, like the full Spartan citizens, the hómoioi, fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>, probably in the same units.</p><p>They could own land, which the army service required. Because Spartans were not permitted economically productive work in the Classical period, the perioeci made weapons and armour and conducted trade, and the masks and figurines found at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-artemis-orthia">Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia</a> were probably made by perioecic craftsmen.</p><p>Like the Spartans they owned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, so that the main division in Spartan society lay between Spartans and perioeci on one side and helots on the other. During the Peloponnesian War, in 413, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> raided the perioecic city of Epidaurus Limera hoping to trigger a helot revolt against the perioeci. Some helots could rise to perioecic status as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neodamodes">neodamodes</a> after military service, but Spartan citizens could not be demoted to it.</p><p>Graham Shipley has identified at least 33 perioecic cities, 23 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and 10 in Messenia. Aithaia and Thouria were the only two to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helot</a> revolt of 464. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> lost the Messenian cities after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> founded Messene in 369 and after Philip II's campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in 338. Other cities, such as Elis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, also controlled perioecic cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s Politics uses the word more generally. In his ideal state, those who perform trades should be slaves or perioeci, because such work does not produce virtue, according to him. In that sense the perioeci are free men who do not own property and cannot vote or hold office, a middle class below the citizens and above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. He sometimes calls them barbarian, implying non-Hellenic foreigners. Ancient authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> name individual perioeci, among them the spy Phrynis, the fleet commander Diniades and the cavalryman Eudicus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Perioeci” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following generations the Spartans subjected the rest of Laconia, whose free inhabitants became <b>perioikoi</b>, dwellers around, with local self-government but no voice in Spartan policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around the citizens lived the free <b>perioikoi</b> in their own towns and several in-between groups: inferiors who had lost citizenship, foster brothers raised beside Spartiate boys, and freed helots.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 6th century BCE the cities formed a league with the neighbouring <b>perioikoi</b> peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/thessalian-league">Thessalian League</a></li></ul></section>
